---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../data/site";

const title = "Quote Attribution Policy";
const description =
  "How Echo Buddha labels original quote reflections, verified quotes, paraphrases, popular sayings, and quote corrections.";
const canonicalPath = "/quote-attribution-policy/";
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: title,
  description,
  url: `${SITE.url}${canonicalPath}`,
  publisher: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: SITE.name,
    url: SITE.url
  }
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath={canonicalPath} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container prose">
      <a class="back-link" href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy</a>
      <span class="eyebrow">Quote integrity</span>
      <h1>Quote Attribution Policy</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Echo Buddha protects quote integrity by treating current quote pages as original Echo Buddha reflections unless
        another source is clearly verified and labeled.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>Current Quote Status</h2>
      <p>
        Current Echo Buddha quote categories and quote stories are original Echo Buddha editorial reflections unless a
        page clearly says otherwise. They are inspired by Buddhist practice themes, but they are not direct scripture
        translations or verified historical sayings.
      </p>
      <p>
        A quote should not be called a Buddha quote, Dhammapada quote, scripture translation, sutta quote, or
        named-person quote unless the origin has been checked against a reliable source.
      </p>

      <h2>Verified Quotes</h2>
      <p>
        If Echo Buddha later publishes a verified quotation, the page should name the source, translator or edition
        where relevant, copyright or license status where needed, and whether the wording is exact, lightly excerpted,
        or translated by someone else.
      </p>
      <p>
        Verified quote labels should be specific. A page should say "Dhammapada source context" or "quoted from a
        named translation" only when that is true.
      </p>

      <h2>Paraphrases and Reflections</h2>
      <p>
        Paraphrases and reflections can be useful, but they must be labeled honestly. A paraphrase restates a source
        idea in new language. A reflection uses an idea as a starting point for original Echo Buddha writing.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a popular saying is common online but uncertain in origin, Echo Buddha should avoid presenting it as a
        verified Buddhist quote. The safer label is "popular paraphrase," "attribution uncertain," or "original
        reflection inspired by Buddhist themes," depending on the evidence.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Quote attribution corrections are welcome. Send the page URL, the quote text, the suspected issue, and any
        source evidence through <a href="/corrections/">Corrections and Editorial Updates</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a quote attribution is wrong or unclear, Echo Buddha may update the label, remove the attribution, add a
        source note, or rewrite the page so readers are not misled.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .back-link {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
</style>
